WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:08.040
User account and activity artifacts help analysts understand who could access a Windows system and what actions may be associated with an account.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.740
Accounts may be local, domain-based, cloud-connected, service-oriented, or temporary.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:21.520
Logon activity can appear in Windows Event Logs, EDR telemetry, remote access logs, identity provider records, and application logs.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.380
A strong investigation compares these sources instead of relying on one login event.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.320
User profiles are rich evidence sources.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:41.140
Recent files, jump lists, link files, desktop artifacts, downloads, browser traces, application data, and shell activity can all help reconstruct what a user account did.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.640
Jump lists and shortcuts may show that a file, folder, or application was accessed.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:53.420
Browser and application traces may show web activity, downloads, cloud sync behavior, or document access.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:59.400
Shellbags, at a high level, can provide clues about folder browsing behavior and viewed locations.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.660
They are useful because they may preserve traces of folders even when the folders are no longer present.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.400
Like other artifacts, they need context and corroboration.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:13.340
A shellbag clue does not automatically prove intent or explain how the activity occurred.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.640
Limits of user attribution are important.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:24.660
A user profile artifact may indicate activity under an account, but the account may have been shared, remotely accessed, compromised, or used by automation.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:34.480
Analysts should connect account artifacts with logons, timestamps, device context, network records, file activity, and business context.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.300
The goal is a defensible conclusion, not an overconfident guess.
